@page "/search"

<CatHead Title="Header search" Tier="@CatHead.Tier1">
    The search box in the <a href="/topbar">topbar</a> and the result panel under it: the script ranks
    against an index the app registers and fills the panel the app wrote, row by row, from its
    templates.
</CatHead>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Search.HeaderSearch" Title="Search box" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--flush">
    <p>
        <strong>Live — type in it.</strong> <code>.search</code> goes at the start of the topbar,
        after the nav toggles and before <code>.topbar-spacer</code>, and is hidden below 720px.
        <code>.topbar-btn--search</code> is the way in there: a topbar button that shows exactly
        while the box is hidden and opens the app's own search &mdash; a page, a sheet, the palette.
        Under <code>.layout--bottombar</code> it steps back below 560px, where the
        <a href="/bottombar">bottom bar</a>'s end cell is the search. <code>data-search</code> hands the box to
        <code>sednaUi.search</code>, which ranks against the index the app registered, implements the
        keyboard and fills the result panel you wrote — below; narrow it with
        <code>style="--search-max: 340px"</code> on the box, and keep the input's placeholder —
        the clear button is shown by CSS on <code>:placeholder-shown</code>.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatSnippet Name="Search/SearchResults.html" Title="Result panel">
    <p>
        <strong>The panel is yours to write</strong>, once, outside <code>.topbar</code>, with a
        <code>&lt;template&gt;</code> per row shape; <code>data-search="&lt;panel id&gt;"</code> on the
        input names it. The script clones the templates, fills their <code>data-*</code> slots with
        text and places the panel under the box, so the empty and the cut-off lines are in your
        language. One <code>.search-item</code> per result — a <code>.search-item-title</code> and a
        <code>.search-item-meta</code> line, with <code>.search-item--sel</code> where the keyboard
        is and <code>.search-status</code> for the busy and empty line. Put
        <code>.sedna-scroll</code> on the panel, use an <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> rather than a button
        so middle-click and &ldquo;open in a new tab&rdquo; work, and keep
        <code>role="option"</code> inside <code>role="listbox"</code> — that is what makes
        <code>aria-selected</code> legal while the box keeps focus through
        <code>aria-activedescendant</code>.
    </p>
</CatSnippet>

<CatSnippet Name="Search/AnchoredSearch.html" Title="Results the app renders">
    <p>
        For an app searching a database itself: leave <code>data-search</code> off the input and
        add <code>.search-panel--anchored</code>, which nests the panel under the box —
        <code>.search</code> is already <code>position: relative</code>, so nothing is measured.
        <strong>The trade:</strong> a nested panel joins <code>.topbar</code>'s stacking context at
        z-index 60, so it sits <em>under</em> a modal backdrop however high its own z-index.
    </p>
</CatSnippet>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Interop.SearchRankFromCsharp" Title="Ranking from C#" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <strong>Live &mdash; this ranks this site's own index.</strong> <code>RankSearchAsync</code> ranks what
        <code>RegisterSearchAsync</code> registered against a query, in the browser, and answers every match best
        first; the dropdown's own cut is not applied, and an empty query answers nothing. A page that was not found
        can offer the pages nearest the words of its address. Register the index and close the panel after a
        navigation with <code>CloseSearchAsync</code> from the layout, as <a href="/interop">From C#</a> shows.
    </p>
</CatExample>
